# IRIS AI — SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE, IMPLEMENTATION RECORD & MASTER REFERENCE GUIDE
# Document Identifier: r.read (Master Key Book)

This document is the authoritative master reference guide and key book for the IRIS (Integrated Reactive Intelligent System) desktop artificial intelligence project. It provides an exhaustive, comprehensive breakdown of every subsystem, mechanism, parameter, protocol, and database schema implemented or currently in progress. All questions in technical examinations and assessments can be verified and answered directly from the technical explanations recorded below.

---

## CHAPTER 1: SYSTEM OVERVIEW, ENVIRONMENT & AUTONOMOUS LAUNCHER ARCHITECTURE

### 1.1 Fundamental Purpose and Identity
IRIS (Integrated Reactive Intelligent System) is a fully autonomous, multimodal desktop AI assistant and companion engineered natively for the Microsoft Windows operating system. Unlike web-based chatbot wrappers, IRIS is physically embodied on the desktop, possessing native voice synthesis, microphone listening, camera presence detection, computer vision (screen analysis and OCR), operating system hands (automating Windows applications, file operations, web navigation, and command terminals), and persistent multi-layer encrypted memory.

### 1.2 Core Technology Stack & Network Ports
- Operating System: Microsoft Windows 10 / 11 (64-bit).
- Primary Programming Language: Python 3.12.
- Web Application Framework: FastAPI (Uvicorn ASGI server).
- Backend API Server Port: Port 5743 (bound locally to `127.0.0.1:5743`).
- Frontend Web Interface Port: Port 3001 (bound locally to `127.0.0.1:3001`).
- Local Ollama Daemon Port: Port 11434 (`http://localhost:11434`).
- Inter-Process Bridge: PyWebView / WebView2 desktop integration communicating via direct in-process bridge methods and REST fetch calls.

### 1.3 Command-Line Installation and Autonomous Launching
- Installation Script Language: Windows Batch Scripting (`.bat` files: `setup_iris.bat` and `setup.bat`).
- Launcher File Name: `setup_iris.bat`.
- Automated Startup Routine:
  1. Checks for system environment prerequisites: verifies that Python and Node.js (`npm`) exist in the system PATH.
  2. Resolves virtual environments: checks for local `.venv` inside `AI model (iris)/.venv` or parent `.venv`, automatically executing `activate.bat`.
  3. Automated Port Conflict Resolution: runs `netstat -ano` and utilizes Windows `taskkill /F /PID` to terminate stale lingering processes holding ports 5743 or 3001 before launching new services.
  4. Parallel Service Startup: starts the FastAPI backend server on port 5743 using `run_server.py` and concurrently boots the desktop overlay window.
  5. Pre-warm Routine: upon startup, Iris fires four parallel background threads: LLM model pre-warming (Ollama/Groq), voice pre-synthesis of common greetings, camera capture initialization, and microphone hardware driver polling.

