## 13. Universal Broadcast Messaging & Visual Studio Code Execution

### 13.1 Universal & Multi-Recipient Messaging Engine (`hands/whatsapp_controller.py`, `hands/social_controller.py`)
- Upgraded communication controllers to support broadcasting to everyone as well as sending messages to multiple designated recipients:
  - Supports natural voice and text commands such as "send message to everyone saying...", "tell everyone that...", "message all contacts...", or "send message to mom, dad and brother saying...".
  - Recognizes synonyms for universal broadcast including "everyone", "every one", "everybody", "all contacts", "all chats", "all friends", and "broadcast".
  - Decider subsystem parses both single recipients, lists of contacts separated by commas or conjunctions, and broadcast directives with zero manual punctuation required.

### 13.2 Broadcast to Everyone & Active Contact Distribution
- Dual-Tier Universal Messaging:
  - Local Contact Book Integration: Checks for structured contact directories or saved groups in data storage. If custom contacts exist, sequentially dispatches the message to all saved recipients.
  - Desktop Client Automation: If no contact list is configured, brings the active WhatsApp window to the foreground, opens the recent conversation list, and delivers the message to the top active contacts.
  - Automatic Clipboard Staging: Copies the message to the Windows clipboard simultaneously so the user can paste it into any broadcast list or status channel with a single keypress.

### 13.3 Sequential Multi-Contact Messaging
- Multi-Recipient Parsing:
  - When the user specifies multiple names (such as "send message to Alice, Bob and Charlie saying meeting started"), the controller breaks down the recipient list into individual targets.
  - Automatically searches each contact, loads the conversation thread, pastes the message, and presses Enter, applying brief pauses between sends to comply with natural desktop interaction timing.

### 13.4 Visual Studio Code Program Runner (`hands/system_control.py`, `hands/task_executor.py`)
- Dedicated In-Editor Execution System:
  - Implements the new intent and action `run_in_code_editor` triggered by phrases such as "run and execute the program in vs code", "run the program in vs code", "execute code in vs code", or "run python file in vs code".
  - Automatic File Resolution: Determines the target file to execute. If the user mentions a specific file name, it locates it. If the user says "the program" or "this code", it automatically identifies the most recently edited code file across the active workspace, project folder, or Desktop.
  - Window Activation & Focus: Launches or restores the Visual Studio Code desktop window and forcefully brings it to the active input foreground using Windows desktop attachment APIs.

### 13.5 Integrated Terminal Automation & Keystroke Triggering
- Visual In-Editor Execution:
  - Focuses the editor window and sends the official VS Code integrated terminal shortcut (Control plus backtick) to toggle or focus the embedded terminal.
  - Automatically detects the programming language runtime (Python, Node.js, PowerShell, or Batch script) and types the exact run command into the integrated terminal, then presses Enter.
  - Simultaneously sends the standard run shortcut (Control plus F5) to activate the editor's native debugger and runner extensions.

### 13.6 Automated Program Output Capture & Companion Reporting
- Dual-Layer Execution Feedback:
  - While the script is visibly running inside VS Code's editor terminal on screen, the system concurrently executes the process in the background with a safety timeout to capture standard output, standard error, and process return codes.
  - The cognitive brain formats the resulting execution output and reports it back to the user via voice and chat response (e.g. "Executed program in VS Code terminal. Output: ...").


